{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for publication-from-a-fold Draft 3 at exact 4025997d12dc41a3e057fea2c7b15c9636acd1cb, primary 911290d3 first. Root directly read the complete 18,759-byte primary through EOF, SHA256 6214b696c9852e24daad7edcfa4e475a0cb0f26d7658a0c86a9073358c02e356. Two independent reviewers read their own complete primary and selected native support. This is a design review; no implementation or execution is approved.\n\nP2 — Make final-gone repair executable. Section 5.3 promises publish on a thing in any state, then permits manual repair when the trial refuses final. The accepted shelf includes retained cancelled pages and Refresh across all things, beyond the 64-live summary. Native judge.ts304–306 refuses a transition on a final primary item. A comment is not a workaround: validate/handlers.ts209–211 forbids comment sends and other item subjects. Specify a supported declared form for the sixth publish act that can repair a gone thing without changing its final state, and require a real gone-item repair witness. A live library/helper target reading the gone thing is a candidate to validate, not a proved solution. Preserve the full Refresh outcome rather than substituting manual Git repair.\n\nP2 — Preserve shelf-path ownership after cancellation. Section 5.1 checks that no live thing has the donated page. Cancel A, then donate B with A's stored path: the stated guard admits B and its publication overwrites A's retained gone page. Random client-generated names reduce accidental collisions but do not enforce non-reuse of a signed field. Define bounded ownership/reservation that covers retired things and witness this sequence refusing the reused path while keeping A's page/history. The accepted library contract retains one page per thing and includes cancelled things in Refresh. Keep Hugh's accepted random paths, unverified content and broad platform authority; this finding concerns the lending definition's own retained shelf contract.\n\nP2 — Define registration recovery before relying on the destination allowlist. Section 9.2 introduces a directory tell that records each created application in the destination. Creation and registration are separate effects. A created child whose registration is interrupted, refused or unknown can remain absent from that allowlist; its new publish act cannot repair missing admission. Specify the completion barrier or directory reconciliation/repair path, including original-send custody, read-only reconciliation of unknown outcomes and an idempotent exact application reference. Do not blindly replay an unknown tell. Require child-created/registration-lost-answer/restart recovery followed by one authorized publication. Add refusals for caller-supplied destination genesis and registration from a wrong/forged directory, besides outside-room publish-file. These are design completeness gaps, not claims of an executed exploit.\n\nArchitecture: a separate cause-bound file-publication item is a sound direction and preserves the change lane's existing operation types. Resolve the three gaps above, then implement the explicit new directory/destination cohort with old rooms retaining their pins. Queue details may be builder criteria: one shared publication slot, defined ordering across lane and application writes, combined bounded admission/owed receipts, and restart behavior that preserves unknown attempts. Require interleaved lane/file and successive shelf-write witnesses; an older completion must not be presented as the newest act's page.\n\nSecurity: Hugh explicitly accepts application writes to any path without review rules. Preserve that decision and document it. The destination's own directory remains the registration authority, and publication must match the full verified application reference/incarnation, not a definition name or caller field. Bind path/content, sender and sending-entry seq/hash to the commit and receipt. The current reserve/name-under admission concerns are separately tracked by 2ce4730e/5e271ec8; later reservation judgment was not fully traced, so this review asserts no complete cross-room exploit.\n\nSimplification: one signed lending act removes browser open/propose/merge custody, but destination delivery and publication still need reconciliation. Retain lending request/envelope/audio-independent custody and distinguish accepted act, page presence, cause-bound receipt, refused and unknown push. Section 4.2's 'page cannot fall behind' must describe payload retention, since section 7 permits push lag/failure. Page presence at HEAD does not prove this act's push completed. Verify maximal escaped UTF-8 content against 4,096 bytes; this is a builder witness, not a demonstrated overflow. Correct the receipt-bound wording: current RECEIPTS_OWED=64 admits the guard until 65 are already owed; section 8's refusal at 64 conflicts with section 9.1/source comments.\n\nThe in-memory trial establishes the recorded validator/rule/send observations only, not factory, authorization, delivery, push or receipt. Hugh's adopted sixth publish act and three consequences remain: client-supplied unchecked text, scope/incarnation in the page with entry linkage through receipts, and fixed random shelf paths. These replace the corresponding older shelf details; all six implementation outputs, public history and final-gone repair remain owed under amended ebda829b/19264feb. No new protocol finality rule or rollback of those accepted decisions is requested.\n\nNormal prepare binds this sole note to bb47000e/82fb8947. Please revise the bounded design and file a fresh exact-head review. Keep existing approved lending-note bytes and receiving work; no gate or provider run is needed for this note-only correction. Supporting Source wholes remain PARTIAL with complete selected units. No checker runtime, tests, gate, install, browser/provider/private proof traversal, Source edits or merge occurred. Root and peer audits are in fold-402-root, fold-402-protocol and fold-402-lending.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:911290d3069e767e4342a8a0c97a1affba56bc22","binding":"assigned","head":"4025997d12dc41a3e057fea2c7b15c9636acd1cb","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d8a7c1ea6f8c193e641e8e50dd42a030cf99d155\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:82fb8947f7dc6006ecb290fc0f4542f1ca34cda4\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f7f383f1da970c96a78658d6d49938c5d2584c5c","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}